  "summary": "Folk craft movement celebrating anonymous, everyday objects made by ordinary artisans.",
  "description": "Emphasizes simplicity, functionality, and natural beauty in handmade objects, reacting against industrialization and valuing traditional crafts.",
  "culturalContext": "As a young man, Yanagi developed a liking for Joseon (1392–1910) ceramics, and in 1916, made his first trip to Korea. There he started to collect items, especially pottery, made by local Korean craftsmen. Realising that Yi Dynasty wares had been made by \"nameless craftsmen\", Yanagi felt that there had to be a similar sort of \"art form\" in Japan. On returning home, therefore, he became interested in his own country's rich cultural heritage and started collecting \"vanishing\" craft items. The objects in his collection included woodwork, lacquer ware, pottery and textiles – from Okinawa and Hokkaidō (Ainu), as well as from mainland Japan.",
